WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.280
I think something here about violence and how we

00:03.280 --> 00:06.120
interviolence resonates with people and the image

00:06.120 --> 00:07.080
and the way you made it.

00:07.080 --> 00:11.520
So I mean, do you have things to offer with this one?

00:11.520 --> 00:13.440
So interesting that that's the one you chose.

00:13.440 --> 00:14.840
I wasn't expecting that.

00:14.840 --> 00:17.840
But I love that.

00:17.840 --> 00:20.640
I mean, to me, this image, I really

00:20.640 --> 00:23.400
wanted to do that quote because it speaks to me

00:23.400 --> 00:28.200
as someone who has, like most of us, all of us,

00:28.200 --> 00:32.080
has deep experience with being on many sides of violence.

00:32.080 --> 00:36.040
And I both had, like many of us who've

00:36.040 --> 00:37.840
been in the movement for a long time.

00:37.840 --> 00:44.440
I mean, there's such destructive, even by many, many people

00:44.440 --> 00:49.800
who are well known as abolitionists, thinkers, and teachers,

00:49.800 --> 00:52.280
and practitioners, how do we actually

00:52.280 --> 00:54.280
practice this in our lives?

00:54.280 --> 00:58.240
Are we ostracizing people in our communities?

00:58.240 --> 01:03.800
Are we allowing for the possibility of transformation

01:03.800 --> 01:10.000
and redemption and sanctuary?

01:10.000 --> 01:12.600
Or are we just saying, especially with people,

01:12.600 --> 01:16.280
like trans people of color and other folks who just

01:16.280 --> 01:18.720
have so little support?

01:18.720 --> 01:21.440
Are we saying, well, go die?

01:21.440 --> 01:23.560
This is the only community where you get to live.

01:24.040 --> 01:27.360
And we no longer welcome you and goodbye.

01:27.360 --> 01:30.720
And I just see that so much and have experienced versions

01:30.720 --> 01:31.600
of that myself.

01:31.600 --> 01:36.600
And I think as an artist, when you talk about what's scary,

01:36.600 --> 01:39.360
I mean, we have to talk about it.

01:39.360 --> 01:43.840
I used to think it was just me, but as I continued for decades

01:43.840 --> 01:45.760
to make art in the movement, privately,

01:45.760 --> 01:48.880
the number of artists and thinkers and just cultural workers

01:48.880 --> 01:52.120
who reach out to me privately and say, OK,

01:52.120 --> 01:54.920
I've had this situation for years with someone

01:54.920 --> 02:01.440
accusing me of terrible things that I did not do.

02:01.440 --> 02:05.000
And honestly, even if you did them,

02:05.000 --> 02:10.320
isn't the point of abolition that you get a chance

02:10.320 --> 02:13.400
that you are not disposable.

02:13.400 --> 02:16.280
So I think artists are especially

02:16.280 --> 02:20.080
a risk for community repribution

02:20.080 --> 02:22.400
because our work is so public.

02:22.400 --> 02:28.280
And I have certainly experienced that.

02:28.280 --> 02:33.160
And the people who are in my life,

02:33.160 --> 02:35.160
who are my informant, who are my family,

02:35.160 --> 02:43.840
who are all of this work has a very deep, informal advisory

02:43.840 --> 02:48.200
committee who are like my family, who say, oh my god,

02:48.200 --> 02:52.920
nope, that analysis is off or her chin looks wrong or whatever.

02:52.920 --> 03:03.040
And yeah, so just to me, this text speaks

03:03.040 --> 03:10.440
to the fact that we all have many different experiences

03:10.440 --> 03:11.680
in relation to violence.

03:11.680 --> 03:15.240
And if we're going to be honest, we need to talk about that.

03:18.520 --> 03:22.920
I also love this piece.

03:22.920 --> 03:25.480
And I teach with this one.

03:25.480 --> 03:27.920
I think I have a lot of students who come.

03:27.920 --> 03:31.920
And I think it's really easy to do this myself.

03:31.920 --> 03:34.360
Who come to you thinking about violence

03:34.360 --> 03:38.360
with that good bad binary with the victim offender binary?

03:38.360 --> 03:43.480
And it's just it's not a sustainable way

03:43.480 --> 03:47.760
to think for the kinds of futures we talk about building.

03:47.760 --> 03:54.240
And I would love for you to explain why a volcano?

03:54.240 --> 03:57.400
And is that water in front of the volcano?

03:57.400 --> 04:01.240
And if so, why that juxtaposition?

04:01.240 --> 04:06.000
Yeah, tell us a little bit more about the process and the rationale.

04:06.000 --> 04:08.760
And if you would read the Sir Ed quote,

04:08.760 --> 04:15.600
which is just foundational to my life,

04:15.600 --> 04:17.720
like this piece is so important.

04:17.720 --> 04:23.040
It's just like that quote is like a guiding beacon in my daily life.

04:23.040 --> 04:25.040
So this piece really resonates with me.

04:25.040 --> 04:29.280
And I'd love to hear you just describe it.

04:29.280 --> 04:34.520
I want to come to your class and listen to teach about this piece.

04:34.520 --> 04:38.480
So the quote says, no one enters violence

04:38.480 --> 04:40.600
for the first time by committing it.

04:40.600 --> 04:52.720
And there's an image of a active volcano erupting

04:52.720 --> 04:55.680
in front of a black story sky.

04:55.680 --> 05:01.400
And the lava is flowing down into those.

05:01.400 --> 05:06.800
And there's something about the fact

05:06.800 --> 05:19.560
that a volcano is a perfect example of how violence can be

05:19.560 --> 05:22.600
part of world building, earth building,

05:22.600 --> 05:31.480
and part of our foundations and can build new land that

05:31.480 --> 05:34.320
can transform and become fertile.

05:34.320 --> 05:36.280
And also how we need the ocean.

05:36.280 --> 05:46.800
We need oceans that can cool and that are very difficult,

05:46.800 --> 05:49.120
hot molten lava can flow into.

05:55.400 --> 05:58.520
So this one's intense.

05:58.520 --> 05:59.560
Yeah.

05:59.560 --> 06:01.240
Thank you.

06:01.240 --> 06:04.600
It sounds intense because it is like a someone

06:04.600 --> 06:10.560
who's been violent and struggles with those kinds of issues.

06:10.560 --> 06:15.640
When I look at this, I'm like, this is how it feels.

06:15.640 --> 06:21.240
And then I keep looking and to have the ocean,

06:21.240 --> 06:26.480
that possibility of whether we think about it as accountability

06:26.480 --> 06:32.280
or cooling off space somewhere to just be safe and like,

06:32.280 --> 06:34.800
yeah, try to.

06:34.800 --> 06:38.040
I love the idea of the molten hardening

06:38.040 --> 06:44.960
and it becoming like this rejuvenating kind of healing process.

06:44.960 --> 06:47.000
Like that was a really beautiful description.

06:47.000 --> 06:48.000
Thank you so much.

06:52.000 --> 06:55.040
When I think the first time I saw it,

06:55.040 --> 06:57.680
and then when I saw it, I mean, my students' reactions in class,

06:57.680 --> 06:59.000
they often choose this one.

06:59.000 --> 07:00.280
They like this one a lot.

07:00.280 --> 07:02.800
And I think it's because I don't know.

07:02.800 --> 07:05.160
I mean, but what they tell me on the basis of what they tell me

07:05.160 --> 07:08.760
on the basis of how I see people using it,

07:08.760 --> 07:11.160
it gives us a space to be seen in who we are,

07:11.160 --> 07:14.560
which is people who commit harm and people

07:14.560 --> 07:16.240
who have harm committed against us.

07:16.240 --> 07:19.840
And so there is a space where we really can be.

07:19.840 --> 07:21.520
And people want that.

07:21.520 --> 07:24.480
I mean, in all this world of binaries,

07:24.480 --> 07:27.160
they want the ways to break through.

07:27.280 --> 07:32.160
And so it does an amazing kind of work for us all.

07:35.640 --> 07:39.600
I think, did you want to add to that, Micah?

07:39.600 --> 07:43.080
Well, I'm still thinking about what, you know,

07:43.080 --> 07:46.200
sort of the behind the scenes, behind the curtain,

07:46.200 --> 07:51.720
kind of ways that artists experience punishment

07:51.720 --> 07:56.600
and the movement for our just practicing

07:56.600 --> 07:59.520
and public, you know, being human

07:59.520 --> 08:04.520
for the ways that art is complex and not literal all the time.

08:07.920 --> 08:12.560
And especially really marginalized artists like, you know,

08:12.560 --> 08:15.160
trans women and trans women artists and other folks,

08:15.160 --> 08:18.280
like, you know, once turf skin onto them.

08:18.280 --> 08:22.640
And it just, it just how much people I know

08:22.640 --> 08:27.640
are suffering and pain for this and not defended

08:28.720 --> 08:30.800
by our movements because of the cancel culture

08:30.800 --> 08:31.640
in our movements.

08:31.640 --> 08:33.440
And I'm really looking forward to a time

08:33.440 --> 08:35.080
in our movements when that changes.

08:35.080 --> 08:36.920
I don't know they'll be in my lifetime,

08:36.920 --> 08:41.240
but, you know, the people don't talk about enough

08:41.240 --> 08:45.080
in my opinion, the cost that movement arts

08:45.080 --> 08:47.440
go through privately for our work.

